<p class="no-indent" style="text-align:center; color:#888; font-size:15px; margin-bottom:30px;">Based on LM I:24 (conclusion)<br/>Parshas HaChodesh ֲ· Chamah &amp; L'vanah ֲ· Chatzos ֲ· Mashi'ach ֲ· Return to N'filas Apayim</p>

<h3>ֲ§20</h3>

<p>Afterward we read <span class="key-term">Parshas HaChodesh</span>. All of this is the secret of <span class="transliteration">Kiddush HaChodesh</span> <span class="bracket-def">[sanctification of the new month]</span> ג€” the tikkun of the moon from its diminishment. The <span class="transliteration">chamah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[sun]</span> and <span class="transliteration">l'vanah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[moon]</span> were created equal: "<span class="transliteration">Es sh'nay ham'oros hag'dolim</span>" ג€” "The two great luminaries" <span class="source">(Beraishis 1)</span>. But through the moon's complaint ג€” "It is impossible for two kings to use one crown" <span class="source">(Chullin 60)</span> ג€” the moon was diminished. Through the mitzvah of Kiddush HaChodesh we work to fill the moon's diminishment.</p>

<p>All this derives from the above principle: whoever merits the hasagah through pursuit and restraint (matai v'lo matai = tachlis haydi'ah asher lo naida) merits filling the moon's diminishment. The light of <span class="bold-concept">chamah and l'vanah derives from the Ain Sof</span> (above nefesh, ru'ach, neshamah) ג€” the source of all luminaries in all worlds. Since this light can only be perceived through the two aspects (pursuit and restraint), two luminaries derive from these two aspects. <span class="key-term">Chamah = ydi'ah</span> (pursuit of the Mochin): "<span class="transliteration">Shemesh yada m'vo'o</span>" ג€” "The sun knows its setting" <span class="source">(Rosh HaShanah 25)</span>. <span class="key-term">L'vanah = absence of ydi'ah</span> (the restraint): the moon does not know its setting.</p>

<p>In their root both are equal ג€” "<span class="transliteration">sh'nay ham'oros hag'dolim</span>" ג€” for through both together one attains the Ain Sof. But the moon grew jealous of the sun and damaged what Adam HaRishon damaged: she did not want to wait, did not want to believe that both would be equal ג†’ she was diminished. This is exactly the p'gam of the l'vanah: her light derives from the restraint; she refused to believe that pursuit and restraint are included together ג†’ she was diminished.</p>

<p>Therefore the essential tikkun of the l'vanah is through <span class="bold-concept">simchas hamitzvah</span> ג†’ attaining the Ain Sof through pursuit and restraint together ג†’ chamah and l'vanah included together, light flowing equally = "<span class="transliteration">sh'nay ham'oros hag'dolim</span>." This is the secret of the <span class="transliteration">ibur</span> <span class="bracket-def">[intercalation of the calendar]</span>, transmitted only to the greatest of Yisrael (deriving from the secret of the nine Haichalos through pursuit and restraint). Therefore the custom is to <span class="key-term">rejoice greatly at Kiddush L'vanah</span> (as the Shulchan Aruch states ג€” performing dances and celebrations) ג€” for the tikkun of the moon's diminishment is essentially through simchas hamitzvah. This is what we say: "<span class="transliteration">Sasim us'maichim la'asos r'tzon konam</span>" ג€” "Joyous and glad to do the will of their Master." And in the blessing over the luminaries on Shabbos: "<span class="transliteration">S'maichim b'tzaisam v'sasim b'vo'am</span>" ג€” "Glad in their going out and joyous in their coming" ג€” the luminaries are essentially through simchah.</p>

<p>Kiddush HaChodesh is the first mitzvah Yisrael were commanded ג€” because Kiddush HaChodesh (tikkun of the moon) depends on simchah (= the root of all Torah mitzvos: "<span class="transliteration">Pikuday Hashem y'sharim m'samchay laiv</span>"). It was commanded at the time of Yetzias Mitzrayim ג€” for the essential geulah is through simchah: "<span class="transliteration">Ki v'simchah saitzai'u</span>."</p>

<h3>ֲ§21</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">chatzos lailah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[midnight]</span> ג€” when the essential geulah of Mitzrayim occurred: "<span class="transliteration">Kachatzos halailah ani yotzai b'soch Mitzrayim</span>" ג€” "At about midnight I go out in the midst of Mitzrayim" <span class="source">(Shemos 11)</span>. Day and night = chamah and l'vanah = pursuit and restraint ג†’ through both together one attains His Elokus in matai v'lo matai = tachlis haydi'ah asher lo naida. There, day and night are included together: "<span class="transliteration">V'hayah yom echad hu yivada laHashem lo yom v'lo lailah v'hayah l'ais erev yihyeh or</span>" ג€” "There will be one day known to Hashem, neither day nor night, and at evening time there will be light" <span class="source">(Z'charyah 14)</span>. In the future we will merit this hasagah ג€” day and night included together (above time).</p>

<p>"<span class="transliteration">V'hayah l'ais erev yihyeh or</span>" ג€” the essential hasagah of the Ain Sof is through the M'sader (= the restraint = the aspect of <span class="key-term">darkness</span>): "<span class="transliteration">Yashes choshech sisro</span>" ג€” "He made darkness His hiding place" <span class="source">(Tehillim 18)</span>. This darkness is above all lights ג€” through it one attains the Ain Sof. Therefore in the order of Creation, <span class="bold-concept">darkness preceded light</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Vay'hi erev vay'hi voker</span>" ג€” "<span class="transliteration">b'raisha chashuchah v'hadar n'horah</span>" ג€” "first darkness, then light" <span class="source">(Shabbos 77)</span>. One can only perceive the light through the darkness: "<span class="transliteration">K'yisron ha'or min hachoshech</span>" ג€” "The advantage of light from the darkness" <span class="source">(Koheles 2)</span>. When one has the power of the M'sader (restraint ג€” knowing that he does not know) ג†’ he can attain what he attains in matai v'lo matai.</p>

<p>This is <span class="key-term">chatzos</span>: the true point of midnight is extremely difficult to perceive ג€” even Moshe did not know the exact moment of chatzos <span class="source">(Berachos 3)</span>. At midnight, the light of day begins to sparkle ג€” the Ain Sof (root of all luminaries) is revealed at chatzos specifically, for there night and day are included together (= pursuit and restraint together) ג†’ His Elokus is revealed. This point cannot be perceived ג€” even Moshe could not know the exact chatzos ג€” for this is tachlis haydi'ah asher lo naida (one cannot pinpoint the instant where ydi'ah and its absence are included together). Chatzos halailah adjoins Parshas HaChodesh ג€” both are one aspect (the secret of ibur / Kiddush HaChodesh / filling the moon = chamah and l'vanah included together).</p>

<h3>ֲ§22</h3>

<p>The Sages asked <span class="source">(Berachos 3)</span>: if Moshe did not know the exact chatzos, how did Dovid know? The answer: a <span class="key-term">kinor</span> <span class="bracket-def">[harp]</span> was suspended above Dovid's bed ג€” when midnight arrived it played by itself, and through this he knew chatzos. <span class="bold-concept">Moshe = chamah</span> ("the face of Moshe was like the face of the sun" <span class="source">(Bava Basra 75)</span>). <span class="bold-concept">Dovid = l'vanah</span> (Malchus Dovid is compared to the moon). The kinor = simchah drawn through the ten types of melody: "<span class="transliteration">Alay asor va'alay navel alay higayon b'chinor ki simachtani Hashem b'fa'olecha</span>" ג€” simchas hamitzvah from Dovid's kinor ג†’ through this one attains the Ain Sof in matai v'lo matai through pursuit and restraint ג†’ through this he knew chatzos (= the secret of Kiddush HaChodesh).</p>

<h3>ֲ§23</h3>

<p>Therefore <span class="key-term">Dovid specifically</span> merited this ג€” for Dovid = Malchus Mashi'ach = <span class="transliteration">is'arusah d'l'satah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Urah ch'vodi urah hanavel v'chinor a'irah shachar</span>" ג€” "Awake, my glory! Awake, lyre and harp! I will awaken the dawn" <span class="source">(Tehillim 57)</span>. The Sages expound <span class="source">(Berachos 4)</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Ani m'orer hashachar v'ain hashachar m'orer osi</span>" ג€” "I awaken the dawn; the dawn does not awaken me" ג€” is'arusah d'l'satah before is'arusah d'l'ailah.</p>

<p>Moshe took Yisrael out of Mitzrayim (the first geulah ג€” before Kabbalas HaTorah) by necessity through is'arusah d'l'ailah alone ג†’ chametz forbidden; he said "<span class="transliteration">kachatzos</span>" ("about midnight") because the exact point of chatzos (= tachlis ha'aliyah) cannot be attained without is'arusah d'l'satah in completeness. At the very point of chatzos the geulah was accomplished ג€” the dough was baked as matzos ("they could not tarry") ג€” if the geulah had been delayed one more instant they would have been swallowed. From this derives the prohibition of chametz on Pesach even in <span class="key-term">mashehu</span> <span class="bracket-def">[the slightest amount]</span> ג€” the secret of that mashehu: they could not tarry even a mashehu. All at the point of chatzos.</p>

<p>Since the illumination/hasagah descended from above without is'arusah d'l'satah, one must run after Him in great chipazon ג€” forbidden to leaven and settle the mind at all (for attempting to settle the mind then = falling, like Adam HaRishon who wished to inquire immediately). The matzah itself is the <span class="transliteration">asvasa</span> <span class="bracket-def">[remedy]</span> (as the Zohar says): through eating matzah seven days and guarding against chametz in mashehu ג†’ we guard our minds from questioning His ways and gazing where we have no permission ג†’ through this very thing is'arusah d'l'satah is created ג†’ afterward we merit receiving Mochin gradually in order ג†’ after Pesach chametz is permitted (each person according to his level merits the M'sader ג†’ no need for chipazon ג†’ illumination of tachlis haydi'ah ג†’ chametz rectified in kedushah through the Keser).</p>

<h3>ֲ§24</h3>

<p>There is only a <span class="key-term">mashehu</span> between chametz and matzah ג€” and this mashehu cannot be perceived. It is the secret of the point of chatzos lailah, the secret of Kiddush HaChodesh, the secret of <span class="bold-concept">tachlis haydi'ah asher lo naida</span>. This secret will not be fully revealed until <span class="key-term">Mashi'ach ben Dovid</span> comes speedily in our days. Then the geulah will be through Torah, through is'arusah d'l'satah, and the illumination of the Keser will shine upon us ג€” which Mashi'ach will attain: "<span class="transliteration">V'ad Atik Yomin m'ta</span>" ג€” "He reached the Ancient of Days" <span class="source">(Daniel 7)</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore then it is said: "<span class="transliteration">Ki lo v'chipazon saitzai'u uvim'nusah lo sailaichun</span>" ג€” "Not in haste will you go out, and not in flight will you travel" <span class="source">(Y'shayah 52)</span> ג€” no need for chipazon! Then Moshe and Dovid will be included together ג€” <span class="bold-concept">Mashi'ach will comprise both</span>: chamah and l'vanah, pursuit and restraint, <span class="key-term">Da'as and emunah</span>. The restraint = emunah = Malchus Dovid (restraining the Mochin from pursuit, relying on emunah). When both are included together ג†’ the essential hasagah of His Elokus ג†’ in the days of Mashi'ach (comprised of Moshe and Dovid) ג†’ the sin of Adam HaRishon rectified in completeness.</p>

<p>All this we will merit through <span class="bold-concept">performing mitzvos with simchah</span> ג†’ the essential geulah: "<span class="transliteration">Ki v'simchah saitzai'u</span>" ג†’ the hasagah above. Fortunate is the one who merits this speedily in our days, Amen!</p>

<h3>ֲ§25</h3>

<p>Let us <span class="key-term">return to the original subject</span>. All of this is the aspect of the secret of <span class="bold-concept">N'filas Apayim</span> ג€” when the ultimate Zivug and Yichud of the upper worlds takes place: attaining the Ain Sof through the inclusiveness of pursuit and restraint.</p>

<p>Therefore <span class="bold-concept">after N'filas Apayim we say Kedushah d'Sidra</span> ג€” then the shefa descends and is drawn below (as known in the Kavanos). The essential drawing of shefa below is through the M'sader of the Mochin (= the restraint). Without the restraint, the Mochin would ascend higher and higher, exiting their vessels and being nullified entirely ג†’ no shefa would descend ג†’ the world would be destroyed, chas v'shalom (= the death of the kings ג€” the vessels could not contain the light ג†’ the lights/Mochin returned upward to their source). But through the M'sader (from Keser) ג€” when the Mochin rush upward, the restraint holds them back ג†’ the pursuit strikes the restraint ג†’ <span class="key-term">the nine Haichalos are formed</span> ("<span class="transliteration">d'lav inun n'horin</span>") ג†’ through them one attains the Ain Sof in ratzo vashov, matai v'lo matai.</p>

<p>Through this, shefa is drawn and descends below: through the pursuit (ratzo) the Mochin are included above; through the restraint (shov) they return ג†’ in their return all <span class="transliteration">hashpa'os</span> and <span class="transliteration">berachos</span> are drawn below. All berachos derive from the Ain Sof (Who gives life to all, the root of all berachos) ג€” and shefa can only be received through the vessels formed by the nine Haichalos (through pursuit and restraint).</p>

<p>Therefore we say <span class="key-term">Kedushah</span> after N'filas Apayim ג€” the Kedushah of "<span class="transliteration">Uva l'Tziyon</span>." <span class="transliteration">Kodesh</span> = the Mochin. Three <span class="transliteration">kedustos</span> <span class="bracket-def">[holy, holy, holy]</span> correspond to the three Mochin included together during the pursuit ג†’ the nine Haichalos formed through pursuit and restraint.</p>

<p>Therefore this Kedushah is called "<span class="key-term">Kedushah d'Sidra</span>" ג€” "<span class="transliteration">d'Sidra</span>" specifically ג€” on account of the <span class="bold-concept">M'sader v'haM'yashev es haMochin</span>. The essential shefa descends below through this precisely: through the M'sader, the Mochin return into their vessels and draw shefa and abundant good for Bais Yisrael. Amen v'Amen!</p>

<p class="no-indent" style="margin-top:30px;text-align:center;color:#888;font-size:14px;font-style:italic;">Note: N'filas Apayim Halacha 5 is included in Hilchos N'si'as Kapayim Halacha 5.</p>

<div style="margin-top:50px;padding-top:20px;border-top:2px dashed #c8a96e;">
<p class="no-indent" style="text-align:center;font-size:20px;color:#7a622e;font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:5px;">Translator's Overview</p>
<p class="no-indent" style="text-align:center;font-size:13px;color:#999;margin-bottom:20px;">The following summary and diagram are not part of the original text.</p>

<p class="no-indent" style="font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;">The concluding six sections bring the entire sweep of LM I:24 to its landing. ֲ§20 maps chamah and l'vanah to pursuit and restraint (ydi'ah and its absence) ג€” the moon's diminishment = the p'gam of not believing both are equal ג€” its tikkun through simchas hamitzvah. ֲ§21 reads chatzos as the ineffable point where day and night (ydi'ah and its absence) unite ג€” darkness is above all light. ֲ§22: Dovid's kinor (simchah) disclosed chatzos to him. ֲ§23 contrasts Moshe (is'arusah d'l'ailah / chamah / chipazon / "kachatzos") with Dovid (is'arusah d'l'satah / l'vanah / "a'irah shachar"). Matzah = the remedy that creates is'arusah d'l'satah. ֲ§24: The mashehu between chametz and matzah = tachlis haydi'ah = will not be revealed until Mashi'ach (who unites Moshe and Dovid, Da'as and emunah). ֲ§25 returns full circle: N'filas Apayim = the ultimate Yichud through pursuit and restraint ג†’ Kedushah d'Sidra = the M'sader ג†’ shefa descends. The word "Sidra" itself = M'sader.</p>

<p class="no-indent" style="font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;margin-top:15px;">With this section, the entirety of N'filas Apayim Halacha 4 (25 sections across six files) is complete. This is by far the most sustained and architecturally ambitious exposition in the collection thus far ג€” a single Torah (LM I:24) unfolded across Purim, Pesach, the Four Parshiyos, Adam HaRishon, chametz/matzah, chamah/l'vanah, chatzos, and Mashi'ach ג€” all held together by the golden thread of simchas hamitzvah and the M'sader v'haM'yashev es haMochin.</p>

